<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Expats: Resale Planning for Hanoi Motorbikes | Hanoi Motorbike Guide</h1>
<p>Every motorbike bought in Hanoi is two transactions wearing one machine, the purchase at the start of the stay and the sale at the end of it, and the expat who plans only the first one has planned half a journey and will discover the other half at the worst possible hour, usually the week of the farewell party. This guide writes the second transaction properly, refuses to invent market prices or durations because the used market keeps its own moods, and ends with the alternative that needs no resale at all, which deserves honest billing rather than a footnote.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk has watched a decade of departures and briefs every buyer on the exit before the entry. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
